What is Git?
Git is a distributed version control system — a tool that tracks every change you make to your files (usually code) over time.  It was created by Linus Torvalds in 2005 to manage the Linux kernel. 

Think of it like a save system for your project.  Every time you git commit, Git takes a snapshot of your project.  You can go back to any snapshot at any time.

Why use it?
Problem without Git	How Git solves it
You break your code and can't go back	Revert to any previous commit
You lose files (hard drive crash, accident)	Recover from any copy — every developer has the full history
Two people edit the same file and overwrite each other	Branch & merge — work independently, combine safely
You don't know who changed what and when	Every commit records who, what, and when
You need to try a risky experiment	Create a branch, experiment freely, delete it if it fails

"Distributed" — what does that mean?
Unlike older systems (like SVN) where there's one central server with the full history, in Git every developer has a complete copy of the project and its entire history on their own machine.  This means:

You can work offline
No single point of failure (if one server dies, everyone else still has everything)
It's fast — most operations happen locally 
In one sentence
Git is the industry-standard tool for tracking changes, collaborating with others, and recovering from mistakes — and it's what you were just learning commands for in the previous steps.

Command	What it does
git init	Creates a new Git repository in your current folder (makes a hidden .git directory that tracks all changes).
git add .	Stages all files in the current directory so Git knows to include them in the next commit.
git commit -m "..."	Saves a snapshot of the staged files with a message describing the change. This is your first commit.
git branch -M main	Renames the current branch to main (the -M flag forces it even if the name already exists). Older Git versions default to master; this ensures you're using main.
git remote add origin <url>	Connects your local repo to a remote repository (e.g., on GitHub) and gives it the nickname origin. You paste the URL from GitHub here.
git remote -v	Verifies the remote was added correctly — shows the fetch and push URLs for origin.
git push -u origin main	Pushes your local main branch to the remote for the first time.  The -u flag sets up tracking, so from now on you can just type git push / git pull without specifying the remote and branch.
Command	What it does
git branch <branch name>	Creates a new branch with the given name (but does not switch to it).
git switch <new_branch_name>	Switches (checks out) to an existing branch. You're now working on that branch.
git switch -c <new_branch>	Creates a new branch and switches to it in one step. The -c flag means "create".
git switch main	Switches back to the main branch.
git branch -d <new_branch>	Deletes the branch. The -d flag only allows deletion if the branch has been fully merged (safe). Use -D to force-delete unmerged branches.
Key difference from before: the older syntax used git checkout <branch> for switching and git checkout -b <branch> for creating + switching. The newer git switch and git branch commands do the same thing but are more explicit and less error-prone.

Command	What it does
git switch main	Switches back to the main branch.
git merge dev	Merges the dev branch into main — takes all the commits made on dev and combines them into main.
git push	Uploads your local main (with the new merged changes) to the remote (GitHub). No flags needed because you already set up tracking with -u earlier.
git pull	Downloads any new commits from the remote and merges them into your local branch. Useful when a teammate has pushed changes you don't have yet.

The flow in plain English:

You finish work on dev
Switch to main
Pull in all that dev work
Push the updated main to GitHub
(Later) Pull again to get whatever others pushed while you were away
git pull = git fetch + git merge in one step. It grabs new remote commits and merges them locally.
